ewValue": "Bálint Alsáni, born around 1330 in Alsán, Hungary, ascended to prominence as a Hungarian Cardinal and served as the influential Bishop of Pécs from 1374 until his death on November 28, 1408. His ecclesiastical career unfolded during a profoundly turbulent era in Central European history, marked by the Great Schism of the Western Church and the complex political landscape shaped by the reigns of King Louis I 'the Great' and later King Sigismund of Luxembourg. Alsáni's elevation to cardinal in 1384 by Pope Urban VI underscored his strategic importance in securing Hungarian loyalty to the Roman obedience amidst the deep divisions within the papacy.

Alsáni's episcopal tenure in Pécs, one of Hungary's most significant dioceses, saw him oversee considerable administrative and spiritual developments. His cardinalate, bestowed by Pope Urban VI, made him Cardinal-Priest of Santa Sabina and positioned him as a crucial diplomatic agent for the Roman pontiff in Central Europe. He actively worked to consolidate support for the Roman obedience, particularly engaging with King Sigismund of Luxembourg following the death of King Louis I. His participation in the papal election of 1389, which ultimately elected Pope Boniface IX, further cemented his role in the politics of the Great Schism.

